---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/url';

// The db area's docs page: one set of commands, one provider per engine. Kept
// in step with skills/toil/db.md, which is the same reference for agents.
const engines = [
  { name: 'SQLite', status: 'integration', note: 'Built into Node. Tested against the real engine.' },
  { name: 'PostgreSQL', status: 'integration', note: 'Pure-JS driver, bundled. Tested against PostgreSQL 17.' },
  { name: 'MySQL / MariaDB', status: 'integration', note: 'Pure-JS driver, bundled. Tested against MySQL 8.4 and MariaDB 11.8.' },
  { name: 'SQL Server', status: 'integration', note: 'Pure-JS driver, bundled. Tested against SQL Server 2022; connect with a read-only login.' },
  { name: 'Oracle', status: 'integration', note: 'Thin mode, no Oracle client. Tested against Oracle Database 23ai Free.' },
];

const commands = [
  { cmd: 'toil db list-schemas', returns: 'The schemas in the database (for SQLite, the attached databases)' },
  { cmd: 'toil db list-tables [--schema <name>]', returns: 'Tables and views in one schema' },
  { cmd: 'toil db describe-table <table>', returns: 'Columns, primary key, indexes and foreign keys' },
  { cmd: 'toil db preview-table <table>', returns: 'A page of rows, read-only' },
  { cmd: 'toil db query <sql>', returns: 'A page of rows from one read-only statement' },
  { cmd: 'toil db execute <sql> --allow-write', returns: 'Rows changed, in one transaction. The only command that writes.' },
];
---

<Layout title="Databases" description="toil's db area: list schemas and tables, describe a table, preview rows and run read-only queries with paging, on SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL / MariaDB, SQL Server and Oracle.">
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="<b>Docs</b> · Databases"
    title="Databases, <em>read-only by default.</em>"
    lead="One set of commands for every engine: list schemas and tables, describe a table, preview rows, and run queries with a row cap and paging. Writes need your opt-in and a flag."
  >
    <p class="crumb"><a href={url('/docs/')}>Docs</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <span>Databases</span></p>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="section--tight">
    <div class="container doc">
      <aside class="doc__facts" data-reveal>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Area</dt><dd><code>toil db</code></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Engines</dt><dd>SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL / MariaDB, SQL Server and Oracle</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Env prefix</dt><dd><code>TOIL_DB_</code></dd></div>
          <div><dt>Needs</dt><dd>Node 24.14 or later</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </aside>

      <article class="doc__body prose">
        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Engines</h2>
          <p>
            Each connection names its <code>engine</code>, and the commands work the same way on all of them. toil is pre-release: SQLite,
            PostgreSQL, MySQL / MariaDB, SQL Server and Oracle are built and their tests run against the real engines, but nothing is on npm yet.
          </p>
          <ul class="engines">
            {engines.map((e) => (
              <li>
                <span class="engines__name">{e.name}</span>
                <span class={`status status--${e.status}`}>{e.status}</span>
                <span class="engines__note">{e.note}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Connecting to SQLite</h2>
          <p>
            There's nothing to install and no credential: toil uses the SQLite that ships inside Node. A connection needs <code>engine</code> and
            the absolute <code>path</code> of an existing database file. toil never creates one.
          </p>
          <div class="codeblock"><span class="card-tag">shell</span>
<pre><span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_ENGINE=<span class="s">sqlite</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_PATH=<span class="s">/srv/data/app.db</span></pre>
          </div>
          <p>Or in your toil config, where you can keep several connections and name a default:</p>
          <div class="codeblock"><span class="card-tag">~/.toil/config.json</span>
<pre>{`{
  "version": 2,
  "connections": {
    "app": { "area": "db", "engine": "sqlite", "path": "/srv/data/app.db" },
    "scratch": { "area": "db", "engine": "sqlite", "path": "/tmp/scratch.db", "writes": true }
  },
  "defaults": { "db": { "connection": "app" } }
}`}</pre>
          </div>
          <p>Pick a connection per call with <code>--connection scratch</code>.</p>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Connecting to PostgreSQL</h2>
          <p>
            toil speaks PostgreSQL's wire protocol in plain JavaScript, so there's no client library or <code>psql</code> to install. A
            connection needs <code>host</code>, <code>database</code> and <code>user</code>; <code>port</code> defaults to 5432, and
            <code>"ssl": true</code> connects over TLS, checking the server's certificate against your operating system's store.
          </p>
          <div class="codeblock"><span class="card-tag">shell</span>
<pre><span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_ENGINE=<span class="s">postgres</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_HOST=<span class="s">db.imagile.dev</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_DATABASE=<span class="s">app</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_USER=<span class="s">reader</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_PASSWORD=<span class="s">&lt;the role's password&gt;</span></pre>
          </div>
          <p>
            The password is a secret: it comes from an env var or the OS credential store (<code>toil connections login &lt;name&gt;</code>),
            never from the config file. toil ignores <code>PGPASSWORD</code>, <code>~/.pgpass</code> and the other libpq settings, so a
            connection only ever reaches the host it names. Connect with an ordinary role that can read what the agent should see, not a
            superuser.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Connecting to MySQL or MariaDB</h2>
          <p>
            One engine, <code>mysql</code>, covers both, over the same plain-JavaScript driver: no client library or <code>mysql</code> CLI to
            install. A connection needs <code>host</code>, <code>database</code> and <code>user</code>; <code>port</code> defaults to 3306.
            The connection's database is also the default schema, and every other database the account can see is a schema too.
          </p>
          <div class="codeblock"><span class="card-tag">shell</span>
<pre><span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_ENGINE=<span class="s">mysql</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_HOST=<span class="s">db.imagile.dev</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_DATABASE=<span class="s">app</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_USER=<span class="s">reader</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_PASSWORD=<span class="s">&lt;the account's password&gt;</span></pre>
          </div>
          <p>
            toil reads no <code>my.cnf</code> or <code>MYSQL_*</code> setting, never sends a password in clear text, and never uploads a local
            file for <code>LOAD DATA LOCAL</code>. Connect with an account that has only <code>SELECT</code> on what the agent should see.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Connecting to SQL Server</h2>
          <p>
            toil speaks SQL Server's wire protocol in plain JavaScript, so there's no ODBC driver or <code>sqlcmd</code> to install. It signs in
            with a SQL Server login. A connection needs <code>host</code>, <code>database</code> and <code>user</code>; <code>port</code>
            defaults to 1433, and <code>"ssl": true</code> encrypts the connection, checking the server's certificate against your operating
            system's store. Without it the login and results cross the network unencrypted.
          </p>
          <div class="codeblock"><span class="card-tag">shell</span>
<pre><span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_ENGINE=<span class="s">sqlserver</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_HOST=<span class="s">sql.imagile.dev</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_DATABASE=<span class="s">app</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_USER=<span class="s">toil_reader</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_PASSWORD=<span class="s">&lt;the login's password&gt;</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_SSL=<span class="s">true</span></pre>
          </div>
          <p>
            SQL Server has no read-only transaction, so connect with a read-only login: one whose database user is only in
            <code>db_datareader</code>. That's what makes the server itself refuse a write. Keep a second connection, with a login that may
            write, for <code>db execute</code>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Connecting to Oracle</h2>
          <p>
            toil uses the Oracle driver in Thin mode, plain JavaScript, so there's no Instant Client or <code>sqlplus</code> to install. A
            connection needs <code>host</code>, <code>database</code> (the service name, such as <code>ORCLPDB1</code>) and
            <code>user</code>; <code>port</code> defaults to 1521, and <code>"ssl": true</code> connects over TCPS.
          </p>
          <div class="codeblock"><span class="card-tag">shell</span>
<pre><span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_ENGINE=<span class="s">oracle</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_HOST=<span class="s">db.imagile.dev</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_DATABASE=<span class="s">ORCLPDB1</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_USER=<span class="s">reader</span>
<span class="p">$</span> export TOIL_DB_PASSWORD=<span class="s">&lt;the user's password&gt;</span></pre>
          </div>
          <p>
            toil builds the connection from those keys alone and reads no <code>tnsnames.ora</code> or <code>TNS_ADMIN</code>. Oracle stores
            unquoted names in upper case, and toil reports them that way (<code>PEOPLE</code>), but finds <code>people</code> when you type
            it. Connect with an account that can't write: on Oracle, a stored function can still write from a read, as below.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Commands</h2>
          <div class="table-wrap">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>Returns</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                {commands.map((c) => (
                  <tr><td><code>{c.cmd}</code></td><td>{c.returns}</td></tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>
            Each is also an MCP tool from <code>toil mcp --areas db</code>, named after the command (<code>db_query</code>), with the same inputs
            and output. Rows come back as arrays in column order, so a join's two <code>id</code> columns both survive.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Paging</h2>
          <p>
            <code>preview-table</code> and <code>query</code> return 100 rows unless you pass <code>--limit</code> (up to 1000). When there are
            more, <code>meta.page.nextCursor</code> holds a cursor: pass it back with <code>--cursor</code> and the same SQL for the next page.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Writes</h2>
          <p>
            Reads run where the engine itself refuses writes: for SQLite, the file opens read-only; for PostgreSQL, every read is a read-only
            transaction that's rolled back. A query that tries to change data fails, whatever it says. <code>VACUUM</code> is refused on reads outright, because <code>VACUUM INTO</code> can create a file even on a
            read-only connection; run it through <code>db execute</code>. One gap remains: a read can still use <code>ATTACH</code> to
            open another existing SQLite file the process can read. On PostgreSQL, <code>COPY</code> and <code>DO</code> are refused on reads, since
            <code>COPY ... TO</code> can write server files; functions such as <code>lo_export</code> still run for a role allowed to call
            them, which is why the role should be an ordinary one. On MySQL and MariaDB, the session and the transaction are both read-only, so DDL is
            refused too; <code>INTO OUTFILE</code> and <code>SET</code> are refused on reads, since they write server files or change the server. On SQL Server,
            every read runs in a transaction that's always rolled back, and a statement the server reports as a write is refused; <code>COMMIT</code>,
            <code>ROLLBACK</code> and <code>OUTPUT</code> are refused on reads outright. A <code>COMMIT</code> hidden in dynamic SQL can still save a write,
            which toil reports but can't undo, so the login should be read-only. On Oracle, every read is a read-only transaction too, and DDL and PL/SQL are refused, and it needs <code>"writes": true</code> on the connection in your own config
            on reads, because Oracle commits DDL and a block can commit inside one; a stored function with an autonomous transaction still
            writes when a query calls it, which is why the account shouldn't be able to write. Only <code>db execute</code> writes, and it needs <code>"writes": true</code> on the connection in your own config
            and <code>--allow-write</code> on the call. Over MCP, the write tool doesn't exist until writes are on.{' '}
            <a href={url('/how-it-works/#safety')}>How writes are gated</a>.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section data-reveal>
          <h2>Time limits</h2>
          <p>
            Every command stops at <code>TOIL_TIMEOUT_MS</code> (two minutes by default) and on Ctrl+C or an MCP cancel. SQLite can only be stopped
            between rows, so a statement that works a long time before its first row finishes in the background. PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQL Server
            are told to cancel the statement on the server, and Oracle is sent a break, which stops a working statement at once. Prefer <code>LIMIT</code> and indexed filters on large tables.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .crumb { margin-top: var(--space-sm); font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--color-faint); }
  .crumb a { color: var(--color-muted); }
  .crumb a:hover { color: var(--color-accent); }

  .doc { display: grid; gap: var(--space-lg); align-items: start; padding-block: var(--space-lg); }
  .doc > * { min-width: 0; }

  .doc__facts { border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: var(--radius-md); background: var(--color-surface); padding: var(--space-md); }
  .doc__facts dl { display: grid; gap: var(--space-sm); }
  .doc__facts dt { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.65rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-faint); }
  .doc__facts dd { margin: 0.2rem 0 0; color: var(--color-ink); font-size: var(--text-sm); overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  .doc__facts code { color: var(--color-accent); }

  .doc__body { display: grid; gap: var(--space-lg); max-width: 68ch; }
  .doc__body > *, .doc__body section > * { min-width: 0; }
  .doc__body h2 { font-size: var(--text-2xl); margin-bottom: var(--space-xs); }
  .doc__body p { color: var(--color-muted); }
  .doc__body p + p, .doc__body .codeblock + p, .doc__body .table-wrap + p { margin-top: var(--space-xs); }
  .doc__body code { color: var(--color-ink); }
  .doc__body .codeblock { margin-block: var(--space-sm); }

  .engines { display: grid; margin-top: var(--space-sm); border-top: 1px solid var(--color-line); }
  .engines li {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) auto; gap: 0.2rem var(--space-sm); align-items: center;
    padding-block: var(--space-xs); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line);
  }
  .engines li > * { min-width: 0; }
  .engines__name { color: var(--color-ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .engines__note { grid-column: 1 / -1; color: var(--color-faint); font-size: var(--text-sm); }

  .table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin-block: var(--space-sm); border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: var(--radius-md); }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  th, td { text-align: left; padding: 0.55rem 0.75rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line); vertical-align: top; }
  th { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.65rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--color-faint); font-weight: 500; }
  td { color: var(--color-muted); }
  tr:last-child td { border-bottom: 0; }
  td code { white-space: nowrap; }
  /* Phones: each command stacks above what it returns, rather than squeezing two columns. */
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    thead { display: none; }
    table, tbody, tr, td { display: block; }
    tr { padding-block: 0.4rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line); }
    tr:last-child { border-bottom: 0; }
    td { border-bottom: 0; padding-block: 0.15rem; }
    td code { white-space: normal; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
  }

  .status {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.4em;
    font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.68rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase;
    border-radius: 999px; padding: 0.1rem 0.55rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-line-strong); color: var(--color-muted);
  }
  .status::before { content: ''; width: 6px; height: 6px; border-radius: 50%; background: currentColor; }
  .status--planned { border-style: dashed; color: var(--color-faint); }
  .status--integration { color: var(--color-accent); }

  @media (min-width: 900px) {
    .doc { grid-template-columns: 16rem minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .doc__facts { position: sticky; top: 88px; }
  }
</style>
